<p>you can stop treating the words <code>document</code>, <code>window</code>, and <code>navigator</code> as mysterious pieces of special syntax. They are JavaScript-visible values. Those values have properties and methods. Those properties and methods connect the JavaScript program to browser-managed capabilities.</p>

<p>For example, a method is not a completely separate category from a property. A <strong>method is a function-valued property used as an operation associated with an object</strong>.</p>

<pre><code>const calculator = {
  add: (a, b) =&gt; a + b
};

calculator.add(2, 3);</code></pre>

<p>The property is <code>add</code>. Its value happens to be a function. Calling that function through the object is commonly described as calling the object's method.</p>

